  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis show the 500 mb zonal \nflow spanning much of the northern tier of the CONUS. Upstream, a \ndeep trough is noted along the Pacific Coast. At the surface, high \npressure holds over Ontario and the Great Lakes. This sets up more \nfavorable conditions for radiative cooling than last night, yielding \ntemps in the upper 20s to mid 30s away from the lakeshores. Tds \nsuggest that lows will be a few degrees colder with interior areas \nreaching into the mid to upper 20s yet this morning.\n\nHigh pressure and zonal flow dominate the remainder of the work week \nwith continued unseasonably warm afternoon temps in the upper 50s to \n60s (10-15 F above normal). Lows will be near to slightly warmer \nthan normal (~5 F above normal) courtesy of partly cloudy to mostly \nclear nights allowing for better radiative cooling processes to \noffset the positive height anomalies and warmer airmass overhead. \n\nA shortwave tracks to the north on Wednesday with an associated weak \nsfc low moving over Hudson Bay. This sends a weak cold front through \nthe area, but likely only results in increased cloud cover or brief \nperiods of sprinkles as model soundings indicate lackluster and \nquick passing moisture. Probability of measurable precip: <15%.\n\nThe better, albeit still low, chances for precip (15-25%) hold off \nuntil late weekend. The aforementioned deep trough along the Pacific \nCoast forms a closed low and spins relatively in place through mid \nweek before moving eastward on Thursday. The now deep trough splits, \nwith the northern stream pivoting northeast to the Canadian Prairie \nby Saturday. This broader troughing pattern then shifts east over \nCanada through early next week when discrepancies arise in the \nresulting sfc pattern. Two main solutions are present. The first \nscenario: a deeper more progressive sfc low closer to the UP. The \nsecond scenario: not quite as strong of a low with a slower and \nfurther north track. Both scenarios set up unseasonably warm temps \nfrom southerly flow (10-20F above normal), but details on winds are \nmurky. Cluster analysis reveals the progressive and gustier solution \nis primarily favored by the GEFS (70%), but does have ~20% of the \nEPS members. This solution could lead to elevated fire weather \nconditions, particularly on Sunday when post-frontal westerly winds \nand subsidence combine for drier and gusty conditions. Widespread, \nappreciable rainfall is not expected.",
